{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$l=Get-Content docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml; $l[1171..1184]; rg -n '\"'test_empty_attestation_state|def _.*card|def _drifted_need' tests/test_gen_open_items.py tests/test_snapshot_readers.py; git diff --name-only\"","aggregated_output":"phase = 3\r\n\r\n[design.LLR-118]\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-049\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"UX-DESIGNER\"]\r\ntitle = \"Generated owner decision surface (registry + attestation depth)\"\r\nmodule = \"project-trajectory/scripts/gen_open_items.py\"\r\ncode_symbol = \"\"\"render/_brief_cards/_brief_card/_uncited_notice/_attestation_cards/verdict_reattest_block/word_diff\"\"\"\r\ndetail = \"\"\"The rendered half of the attestation regime under SR-049. The page renders, in this order: the pending open items that queued work items cite in their needs, as decision briefs beside the queued rows citing them, preceded by an integrity notice naming and linking the pending items that no queued row cites, the page claiming an empty queue only when there is neither; every SR whose chain holds a row owing a first approval or drifted from the approved snapshot, with the chain's per-cell before/after, a section with no changed cell stating that no cell differs from the approved snapshot and never reading as nothing changed; the audit list of every act-ledger entry naming a verdict, newest first, or None recorded; the pending owner actions; and, last, the Decisions to review section. It owns no second opinion: the queue is pending.open_item_queue, the attestation model trace.reattest_model, the pointers pending.pending_block and the decisions pending.decisions_to_review, imported rather than re-derived, so where this view and the trace.py --approve brief disagree, this view is the defect. NOT claimed: that the rendered page is legible or well-composed.\"\"\"\r\nrationale = \"\"\"A human cannot bless a delta they cannot see, and the depth that makes a re-attest readable is a WORD-LEVEL diff — which the markdown surface this replaces could not mark, so a reviewer was asked to sign a change they could only read as a whole cell. Re-deriving the attestation model here was rejected: a second opinion that disagreed with the brief would leave the reviewer arbitrating between two surfaces. Both emitted contracts exist because their absence fails SILENTLY in front of someone about to attest, which is the worst place for a quiet failure.\"\"\"\r\ntest_refs = \"(see TC-123)\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-009\"\r\nphase = 4\r\ntests/test_snapshot_readers.py:683:def _card(page, rid):\ntests/test_snapshot_readers.py:696:def _drifted_need(root):\ntests/test_gen_open_items.py:582:def test_empty_attestation_state_names_only_what_it_checked(tmp_path):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
